## HOW TO CHOOSE

- **Fewer elements, better chosen.** Three is usually the ceiling: one
  reading, one shape, one roll. A screen with six elements is a screen nobody
  reads.
- **The roll band is not optional.** Whatever else is on the screen, a person
  needs to see the actual rows. Always include exactly one roll element.
- **Prefer the element that engaged with the data.** A calendar over rows with
  a real date beats a table of the same rows; a table beats a list when the
  fields are worth comparing column to column.
- **Skip a band rather than fill it badly.** If nothing divides these rows in
  a way worth showing -- no category, no meaningful split -- omit the shape
  band. An empty pie chart is worse than no pie chart.
- **A chart of a handful of rows is not a chart.** Below roughly ten rows,
  prefer the reading and the roll.
- **`bindings` is for overruling a field choice, and rarely needed.** The
  bindings shown under each candidate are what it would use if you say
  nothing. Name a slot only when a different field is clearly better -- for
  instance a timeline that should plot a business date rather than the row's
  creation time. An EMPTY list declines a slot: use it to suppress a measure
  that is numerically valid and semantically meaningless (a version counter,
  an epoch).
- **`title` is for when the element's own name is not what this screen is
  about.** Most of the time leave it out.
- **A layout is a claim that the screen has a shape.** If the elements do not
  divide into a lead and a supporting cast, or into a list and a detail, say
  `stack` and let the bands speak. A dashboard of one tile and one table is a
  stack with extra CSS.
- **Never remove what the screen is FOR.** If the arrangement you were given
  has a list of the rows, yours has one too. Rearranging a screen is not the
  same as changing what it is about, and an arrangement that dropped the
  population is repaired before it is stored -- so it costs a round trip and
  changes nothing.

## REASONING

Write it for the person about to accept or reject your suggestion, in their
terms: what the arrangement lets them see, and what you left out and why. Not
a description of the JSON.
